      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Exogenous kappa-opioid agonists have been shown to produce peripheral antinociceptive effects in inflamed tissue. This study sought to determine whether endogenous kappa-receptor ligands are present at the site of inflammation. In Freund's adjuvant-induced hindpaw inflammation in the rat, we show, by immunohistochemistry, that dynorphin is detectable within inflammatory cells and in the cutaneous nerves in a similar distribution as calcitonin gene-related peptide, a specific marker for sensory neurons. These findings extend our previous observations in that not only beta-endorphin and Met-enkephalin (mu- and delta-receptor ligands), but also a preferential kappa-ligand is present within inflamed subcutaneous tissue."},
